// Parole di base: the everyday core deck (REVIEW-fluency.md, B1).
//
// The course's own 259 cards are almost all Bible nouns. Reading research puts
// comfortable comprehension at 95-98% of the words on the page, and everyday
// speech needs the small set of very frequent words that make up most of any
// text: prepositions, pronouns, connectors, the twenty verbs that do most of the
// work, and the vocabulary of ordinary life. This deck supplies them.
//
// Each row is [it, en, example, exampleEn, form?, src?]. `form` is the inflected
// form the headword takes in its example ("andare" -> "vado"), so cloze blanks
// the word the learner actually sees. The examples are plain everyday Italian,
// not Scripture, so they carry no CEI text.
//
// Where the examples come from (REVIEW-fluency.md, "How the core deck's Italian
// was checked"). A row with `src` 'tatoeba:<it id>/<en id>' uses a sentence
// written by a native Italian speaker on Tatoeba (tatoeba.org, CC BY 2.0 FR),
// copied verbatim, with its linked English translation; a trailing '*' marks an
// English line we reworded, and 'tatoeba:<it id>' alone means the Italian is
// on Tatoeba but the English is ours. Open https://tatoeba.org/sentences/show/<id>
// to see it. A row without `src` is our own sentence, used where no native sentence
// showed the card's sense (`piano` as "slowly", `la voglia` in "avere voglia").
// `node scripts/check-core-italian.mjs` checks every row either way.
//
// Rules for adding a row (coreVocab.test.js enforces them):
// - the term must not already be a course vocab term (any case);
// - every word of the example must have a gloss (glossCoverage.test.js);
// - the first occurrence of the cloze answer in the example must be a whole
//   word, so the blank never cuts a word in half;
// - prefer a native sentence: `node scripts/check-core-italian.mjs --candidates`
//   proposes Tatoeba sentences for each row; check the Italian of any other
//   sentence carefully. A wrong example is worse than none.

